From: programista-zacny <5969291+programista-zacny@users.noreply.github.com>
Subject: [PATCH] selinux: enable genfscon labeling for sysfs and pstore files

Support per-file labeling of sysfs and pstore files based on genfscon
policy entries, as the tree already does for proc and debugfs. Until now
"genfscon sysfs <path>" rules were ignored: every sysfs node started as
"sysfs" and only got another type when ueventd's restorecon found a
file_contexts entry for it. Current Android policy labels sysfs almost
entirely through genfscon and has few sysfs file_contexts entries left.

The label is computed from the node's path inside sysfs when the dentry
is instantiated, by longest genfscon prefix. Symlinks are skipped
(!S_ISLNK in inode_doinit_with_dentry()), so a rule only applies to the
real path under /sys/devices, /sys/module, /sys/power, ... and to the
/sys/class/<name> and /sys/bus/<name>/devices directories themselves, not
to the links inside them.

A label set from userspace still wins and still sticks: sysfs keeps it in
the sysfs_dirent (sysfs_setxattr()) and replays it through
security_inode_notifysecctx() when the inode is set up again, which marks
the inode initialized before inode_doinit_with_dentry() gets to it. So
file_contexts entries keep the effect they have today.

This changes labels that are in use. With the policy of the current
gts210ltexx build, nodes that no file_contexts entry covers move from
"sysfs" to the type of the matching genfscon rule, and a domain that
reaches such a node only through its blanket "sysfs" rules loses access.
Known cases on this device:
  /sys/devices/virtual/sec/gps (sysfs_sec_gps): gpsd follows
      /sys/class/sec/gps/GPS_PWR_EN/value (gps.xml) through it and has
      no rule for sysfs_sec_gps; needs at least
      "allow gpsd sysfs_sec_gps:dir search;"
  /sys/class/switch, /sys/devices/virtual/switch (sysfs_switch):
      hwcomposer.exynos5 opens /sys/class/switch/hdmi/state;
      hal_graphics_composer_default has no rule for sysfs_switch
  /sys/power/cpufreq_max_limit (sysfs_power_writable): libbauthserver
      (hal_fingerprint_default) opens it and has no rule for the type
Add those rules to the device policy together with this patch and check
the first boot for new avc denials on tclass=file/dir/lnk_file with a
sysfs_* tcontext.

Derived from upstream:
  8e014720 "selinux: enable genfscon labeling for sysfs and pstore files"

Adapted for 3.10: none needed in the code. SE_SBGENFS and the debugfs
case (134509d54e4e "selinux: enable per-file labeling for debugfs
files.") are already in the tree. sysfs here is the pre-kernfs
implementation; it instantiates dentries through d_materialise_unique(),
which calls security_d_instantiate() like the other paths, and
dentry_path_raw() yields the path relative to the sysfs root.

Not compile-tested.
---
 security/selinux/hooks.c | 4 +++-
 1 file changed, 3 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/security/selinux/hooks.c b/security/selinux/hooks.c
index 4db0bd6..4f8967e 100644
--- a/security/selinux/hooks.c
+++ b/security/selinux/hooks.c
@@ -736,7 +736,9 @@ static int selinux_set_mnt_opts(struct super_block *sb,
 	if (strcmp(sb->s_type->name, "proc") == 0)
 		sbsec->flags |= SE_SBPROC | SE_SBGENFS;
 
-	if (strcmp(sb->s_type->name, "debugfs") == 0)
+	if (!strcmp(sb->s_type->name, "debugfs") ||
+	    !strcmp(sb->s_type->name, "sysfs") ||
+	    !strcmp(sb->s_type->name, "pstore"))
 		sbsec->flags |= SE_SBGENFS;
 
 	/* Determine the labeling behavior to use for this filesystem type. */
